328 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Attention divides this 'finding words to focus on' process into three roles: Query, Key, and Value. It's similar to the process of finding a book in a library.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3 4]
>>
endobj
329 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 945 0 R
/E (Query \(Q\): 'The information I'm looking for now'. You can think of it as the word you're currently processing. \(Example: I want to figure out the meaning of the word 'did'.\))
/Alt ()
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5 6]
>>
endobj
330 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 946 0 R
/E (Key \(K\): 'The index or keyword of the book'. It's a unique feature or identifier that all other words in the sentence have. The Query is compared to these Keys and finds out how related they are. \(Example: 'I', 'yesterday', 'at school', 'homework', other words in the sentence.\))
/Alt ()
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
331 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 947 0 R
/E (Value \(V\): 'The actual content of the book'. It's the actual information value linked to the Key. When the Query calculates the weight through the similarity with the Key, the weight is multiplied by the Value and reflected in the final result.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [8 9]
>>
endobj
